spec:
  grid: always 14x14
  colors: 5 (gray), 8 (azure), 9 (maroon) for frame; 7 (orange) for inner fill; 2 (red) marker; 6 (pink) trail
  inner_rect:
    size: 9-12 rows x 9-11 cols, filled with orange (7) plus red marker and pink trail
    position: offset from grid edges by border band widths
  frame:
    four_bands: top/bottom (horizontal) and left/right (vertical), each 1-3 cells thick
    band_structure: each band is uniform across its thickness; along its length it is segmented into 1-4 contiguous runs of frame colors (5/8/9)
    dominant_color: each band has one majority color; each of {5,8,9} is dominant on at least one band; one color is dominant on exactly two bands [observed]
    segments_per_band: 1-4 color runs along band length; total across all 4 bands is 9-13
    corners: where bands overlap, the corner takes the color of one adjacent band
  marker:
    red: exactly 1 pixel inside the inner rect
    pink_trail: 4 pixels in a straight 8-connected line from red (any of 8 directions including diagonal)
    trail_clearance: trail end is 1-2 cells from inner rect edge (in trail direction)
  ray:
    direction: opposite of trail direction (away from pink trail)
    traversal: 1-6 orange cells between red and first non-orange pixel hit
    hit_target: always a frame band pixel (outside inner rect); gets recolored to orange (7) in output
  output: identical to input except the one hit pixel becomes orange

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick inner rect size (9-12 x 9-11) and border band widths (1-3 each, summing to 14 with inner rect per axis).
    2. Choose a random 8-connected direction for the trail. Place red inside inner rect such that 4 pink trail cells fit inside, and the ray (opposite direction) crosses 1-6 orange cells before exiting the inner rect to hit a frame band.
    3. Fill inner rect with orange (7), place red (2) and pink (6) trail.
    4. Assign 3 frame colors {5,8,9} to 4 bands (one color used on 2 bands). Segment each band into 1-4 contiguous color runs; fill corners consistently with one adjacent band.
    5. Identify the frame pixel hit by the ray (first frame cell in ray direction from red). This is the output state (pixel = orange).
    6. To get the input, set that pixel back to its frame band color.
